  --body "## jetson-j2 device benchmark + portable kernel work

### Phase 1: Device benchmark (ob-8ms.3)
- Built and ran the GDN kernel microbenchmark on **Jetson Nano (Cortex-A57, Armv8.0, NEON only)**
- Governor: \`performance\` (all 4 cores at 1479 MHz), CPU thermal stable at 43°C
- Results in \`results/raw/jetson-j2.csv\` with full provenance in \`results/manifests/jetson-j2.json\`
- Key numbers (Qwen3.5-4B shapes, NEON dispatch):
  - \`gdn_gated_scan\`: 0.80 GiB/s p50
  - \`gdn_cumdecay\`: 1.04 GiB/s p50
  - \`gdn_causal_dwconv1d\`: 0.94 GiB/s p50

### Phase 2: fp16/bf16 mixed-precision state variants (ob-8qt.4)
- Added \`gdn_gated_scan_f16\`, \`gdn_cumdecay_f16\`, \`gdn_gated_scan_bf16\`, \`gdn_cumdecay_bf16\`
- **Mixed precision**: persistent state narrowed to fp16/bf16, accumulator stays fp32 (critical: decay of 0.5\^64 ~5e-20 underflows fp16)
- NEON path uses native \`vcvt\`; SVE uses stack-buffer conversion; bf16 uses software round-to-nearest-even
- Correctness verified on A57:
  - fp16-state scan: max_abs=1.07e-4, max_rel=0.95%
  - bf16-state scan: max_abs=8.49e-4, max_rel=4.6%
  - **Multi-chunk drift plateaus at ~4e-4 — gating damps error, no compounding**
- Benchmark: negligible scan overhead; fp16 cumdecay faster; bf16 cumdecay slower on A57 (no hw bf16)

### Commits
- \`0977d34\` j2: device-fleet bench jetson-j2 (ob-8ms.3)
